#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66b3b284-8ce7-592d-ad66-af8680d66ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339820} {:answer/id #uuid "ca042d97-9382-5757-9565-f4c9a592ef0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339821} {:answer/id #uuid "579300e3-dcc6-534e-a548-566e75171ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339822} {:answer/id #uuid "87d55e48-85da-575a-8f45-e51f73e826fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339823} {:answer/id #uuid "90d86725-c631-527d-bfaa-25e158c8f879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "579300e3-dcc6-534e-a548-566e75171ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339822} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea096e98-8fd9-55ff-82e5-2cb9154735dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8708f396-8f97-549d-8100-2f26c28b54d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-125585" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
